+\subsection*{Extension to all finite uniformities}
+The finite linear trace argument can be iterated beyond triples.  For a
+finite graph $J$ and an integer $r\ge3$, write $J^{[r]}$ for the hypergraph
+obtained by adding $r-2$ distinct private vertices to every edge of $J$.
+Let $\mathcal B_r$ be the class generated by these expansions of finite
+bipartite graphs and by finite edgeless systems, under finite disjoint
+unions and one-point amalgamations.  As throughout the paper, embeddings
+are injective and non-induced, and proper vertex colourings have no
+monochromatic edge.
+
+\begin{theoremB}
+For every fixed integer $r\ge3$ and every finite simple $r$-uniform
+hypergraph $F$, the following are equivalent:
+\begin{enumerate}
+\item $F$ occurs in every $r$-uniform hypergraph of uncountable chromatic number;
+\item $F\in\mathcal B_r$;
+\item after isolated vertices are removed, $F$ is linear, each hyperedge-node
+of its Levi graph is incident with at least $r-2$ bridges, and every
+Berge cycle has even length.
+\end{enumerate}
+\end{theoremB}
+
+Theorem~\ref{uniform-classification} proves this extension.  Reiher's
+expansion theorem already supplies the positive generators for every
+uniformity \citep[Theorem~1.2]{reiher2024}.  The additional step is the
+finite linear trace lemma with a hypergraph base: projection preserves
+linearity, and one-private-vertex inflation commutes with the forest
+assembly.  Iterating the lift therefore reduces the negative direction to
+the same high-odd-girth graph input used for triples.  The bridge
+requirement increases with $r$; the example in
+Remark~\ref{uniform-four-edge-example} shows why one bridge no longer
+suffices at uniformity four.
+
+The canonical atoms in this generality are single $r$-edges and
+$J^{[r]}$ with $J$ a finite $2$-connected bipartite graph.  Writing
+$s=n-(r-2)m$ and $\beta=(r-1)m-n+c$ transports the finite spectra,
+factorization products and fixed-atom profiles below to every finite
+uniformity (Corollary~\ref{uniform-finite-spectra}).  The detailed
+triple-system argument and its finite structure remain in Sections~1--10.
+
+\subsection*{Prescribing the chromatic cardinal}
+The structural answer does not depend on which uncountable chromatic
+cardinal is prescribed.  The next theorem strengthens the negative
+direction from an avoiding host to a host of any specified uncountable
+chromatic number.
+
+\begin{theoremC}
+Fix a finite integer $r\ge3$ and a finite simple $r$-uniform
+hypergraph $F$.  If $F\notin\mathcal B_r$, then for every uncountable
+cardinal $\kappa$ there is an $F$-free $r$-uniform hypergraph $H$
+with $\chi(H)=\kappa$.  If $F$ is linear, one may also require
+$|V(H)|\le2^{<\kappa}$.  If $F\in\mathcal B_r$, no such host exists.
+\end{theoremC}
+
+Here $2^{<\kappa}=\sup_{\nu<\kappa}2^\nu$, with the supremum
+taken over cardinals.  Theorem~\ref{exact-avoidance-spectrum} proves
+the assertion, including singular $\kappa$.
+Li established the triple-system spectrum \citep[Theorem~7.1]{li2026}.
+For linear forbidden systems in higher uniformity, the key identity is
+$\chi(\mathcal L_\kappa(H))=\min\{\chi(H),\kappa\}$ for the
+sequence lift of height $\kappa$.  It combines exact chromatic control
+with the finite trace theorem already used for the classification.
+
+Nonlinear forbidden systems require a different construction.
+We extend Li's reservoir method \citep[Section~6]{li2026} to add several
+old vertices to a labelled base edge.  With the simultaneous-colour
+systems of Erd\H{o}s--Galvin--Hajnal \citep[Corollary~12.3]{egh1975},
+this gives, for $1\le i\le r-2$, an $r$-uniform host with pairwise
+edge intersections at most $i$, chromatic number exactly $\mu^+$,
+and order at most $\beth_{\lceil(r-1)/i\rceil}(\mu)$.
+Here $\beth_0(\mu)=\mu$ and $\beth_{j+1}(\mu)=2^{\beth_j(\mu)}$.
+The order bounds are in ZFC; the corresponding optimal GCH thresholds
+are classical consequences of Erd\H{o}s--Galvin--Hajnal.
+
+The same lift avoids any finite family of linear non-obligatory systems
+simultaneously.  This family theorem has a real boundary: for every
+uniformity there is a finite mixed family whose members are individually
+avoidable at every uncountable cardinal, while their simultaneous
+omission forces countable chromatic number
+(Proposition~\ref{exact-mixed-family-obstruction}).
+The new results in Sections~\ref{uniform-extension}
+and~\ref{exact-spectra-extension} have manuscript proofs; they are
+not additional Lean-verified endpoints.
+
+\subsection*{Obligatory atoms and finite density inequalities}
+The atom description leads to a further question: does obligatoriness
+impose the lower bound on copies predicted by a random host of the
+same density?  The next result separates these properties within a
+single cyclic atom.
+
+\begin{theoremD}
+Assume the rank-layer limit in OpenAI's
+\emph{A counterexample to Sidorenko's conjecture} \citep{openai161}.
+There is an explicit $2$-connected bipartite graph $J$ with $35$
+vertices and $66$ edges such that its private expansion $J^{[r]}$
+is not Sidorenko for any finite $r\ge2$, with $J^{[2]}=J$.
+For every $r\ge3$, the expansion is one canonical obligatory atom
+with $66$ edges, $66r-97$ vertices and cyclic rank $32$.
+\end{theoremD}
+
+Here a finite $r$-uniform pattern $F$ is Sidorenko if every finite
+host $G$ satisfies $t(F,G)\ge p_r(G)^{|E(F)|}$, where $t$ counts all
+homomorphisms and $p_r(G)=r!|E(G)|/|V(G)|^r$.
+Nie--Spiro conjectured that every bipartite graph admits a Sidorenko
+expansion in some uniformity \citep[Conjecture~5.1]{niespiro2025}.
+Theorem~D supplies a counterexample under the stated rank-layer input.
+
+The graph and its density deficit originate in \citet{openai161}.
+The new transfer uses more than that deficit: in the source's
+mean-one kernel family, every proper edge-subgraph of $J$ has density
+tending to one.  We prove this additional limit and use an average
+over matchings to realize the required graph marginals by symmetric
+$r$-kernels.  Section~\ref{density-extension} gives these proofs and
+a finite-density separation witnessed by $C_4^{[r]}$.
+The long finite-field estimate remains a cited preprint input;
+the transfer has not been formalized in Lean.
+
+Two unconditional results sharpen this framework.
+Theorem~\ref{density-universal-profiles} characterizes the universal
+realization of arbitrary collections of pair kernels by the matching
+polytope, and proves that the symmetric transfer coefficient is optimal.
+For any graph $F$ with $m$ edges, Theorem~\ref{density-uniform-approximation}
+gives the explicit two-sided estimate
+\[
+ \bigl|t(F^{[r]},U)-p^m\bigr|
+ \le m(m-1)p(1-p)/r
+\]
+for every symmetric $[0,1]$-valued $r$-kernel $U$ of mean $p$.
+For regular kernels, Theorem~\ref{density-regular-girth} improves the
+error to $O_F(r^{-g})$ when the core has girth $g$, and gives exact
+equality for forests.  Explicit kernels of density $1/2$ show that
+these powers of $r$ are optimal.  These results concern quantitative
+homomorphism densities and require no rank-layer hypothesis.
+
