+\section{The classification in every finite uniformity}
+\label{uniform-extension}
+
+The same structural criterion extends to every finite uniformity, with the
+number of required bridge incidences increasing with the edge size.  The
+positive expansions are supplied by Reiher's theorem for all uniformities
+\citep[Theorem~1.2]{reiher2024}.  For the negative direction, we iterate the
+one-apex construction from Section~\ref{the-sequence-lift-used-in-the-avoidance-constructions}
+and \citet[Sections~3--4]{li2026}, allowing a hypergraph of arbitrary finite
+uniformity at its base.  The decisive point is that a finite linear trace
+projects to finite \emph{linear} base systems at every stage of the iteration.
+
+Fix an integer $r\ge3$.  All $r$-uniform hypergraphs below are simple, and
+embeddings are injective and non-induced.  A proper vertex colouring has
+no monochromatic edge.  For a finite graph $J$, let
+$J^{[r]}$ be its $r$-uniform private expansion: add $r-2$ new vertices to
+each graph edge, with all these added vertices distinct.  Thus
+$J^{[3]}=J^+$.  Let $\mathcal B_r$ be the class generated, up to isomorphism,
+by $J^{[r]}$ for finite bipartite graphs $J$ and by finite edgeless systems,
+under finite disjoint unions and one-point amalgamations.  The Levi graph,
+Berge cycles and isolated reduction have the same definitions as before,
+with each hyperedge-node now of degree $r$.
+
+\begin{theorem}[Classification in finite uniformity]
+\label{uniform-classification}
+For every integer $r\ge3$ and every finite $r$-uniform hypergraph $F$, the
+following are equivalent:
+\begin{enumerate}
+\item $F$ occurs in every $r$-uniform hypergraph of uncountable chromatic number;
+\item $F\in\mathcal B_r$;
+\item $F^\circ$ is linear, every hyperedge-node of $I(F^\circ)$ is incident
+with at least $r-2$ bridges, and every Berge cycle of $F^\circ$ has even length.
+\end{enumerate}
+\end{theorem}
+
+For $r=3$ this is Theorem~A.  At $r=2$, the bridge requirement becomes
+vacuous and the cycle condition is the classical bipartite characterization
+\citep{erdoshajnal1966}.  We first prove the trace statement needed for the
+higher-uniformity negative direction.
+
+\subsection{A lift with a hypergraph base}
+\label{uniform-hypergraph-lift}
+
+If $D$ is a $k$-uniform hypergraph, write $D^+$ for its
+$(k+1)$-uniform \emph{one-private-vertex inflation}: each edge receives one
+new vertex, used by no other edge.  On graphs this agrees with the expansion
+notation used earlier, and $(J^{[k]})^+\cong J^{[k+1]}$.
+
+Let $H=(X,A)$ be a $k$-uniform hypergraph, where $k\ge2$ and $A=E(H)$ is
+regarded as an alphabet.  Put $T=A^{<\omega_1}$ and write
+$\ell(s)=\operatorname{dom}(s)$ for the ordinal length of $s\in T$.
+Define
+$\mathcal L(H)$ on $T\times X$ by including exactly the edges
+\begin{equation}\label{uniform-lift-edge}
+ \{(s,x):x\in a\}\cup\{(t,z)\},
+ \qquad a\in A,\quad s^\frown a\preccurlyeq t,\quad z\in X,
+\end{equation}
+where $\preccurlyeq$ denotes the prefix order.  The $k$ vertices at $s$ are
+the base and the vertex at $t$ is the apex.  Since $t$ properly extends $s$,
+these are $k+1$ distinct vertices.  The node occurring $k\ge2$ times
+uniquely identifies the base, so each edge has unique defining data.
+
+\begin{lemma}[Chromatic lower bound for the rank-raising lift]
+\label{uniform-lift-chromatic}
+If $\chi(H)>\aleph_0$, then $\chi(\mathcal L(H))>\aleph_0$.
+\end{lemma}
+\begin{proof}
+Suppose that $c:T\times X\to\omega$ is proper.  At each $s\in T$, its
+restriction to $\{s\}\times X$ has a monochromatic $H$-edge $a_s$, of
+colour $q_s$.  Recursively set $s_0=\varnothing$ and
+$s_{\alpha+1}=s_\alpha^\frown a_{s_\alpha}$, taking unions at countable
+limit ordinals.  For $\alpha<\beta<\omega_1$, the node $s_\beta$ extends
+$s_\alpha^\frown a_{s_\alpha}$.  If
+$q_{s_\alpha}=q_{s_\beta}$, any vertex of the monochromatic base edge at
+$s_\beta$ can serve as an apex for the base edge at $s_\alpha$, producing
+a monochromatic edge of $\mathcal L(H)$.  Thus the colours $q_{s_\alpha}$
+are pairwise distinct for $\alpha<\omega_1$, a contradiction.
+\end{proof}
+
+\begin{lemma}[Finite linear traces over a hypergraph base]
+\label{uniform-lift-trace}
+Let $k\ge2$ and let $H$ be a $k$-uniform hypergraph.  Every finite linear
+subhypergraph $K\subseteq\mathcal L(H)$ is obtained by disjoint unions and
+one-point amalgamations from systems $D^+$, where $D$ is a finite linear
+subhypergraph of $H$, and finite edgeless systems.  Each such $D$ has at most
+$|E(K)|$ edges.
+\end{lemma}
+\begin{proof}
+Set aside the isolated vertices of $K$.  For each node $s$ supporting at
+least one selected edge, let $K_s$ consist of the edges of $K$ based at $s$
+and all their incident vertices.
+
+\proofpart{The fibre pieces}
+No two distinct edges of $K_s$ can use the same base $a\in E(H)$: they
+would share $k\ge2$ vertices, contrary to linearity.  Let $D_s\subseteq H$
+consist of the bases used at $s$, with their incident vertices.  Two of
+these bases meet in at most one vertex, because their corresponding
+$K$-edges do.  Thus $D_s$ is linear.  Moreover, distinct bases have distinct
+apices.  If a point $(t,z)$ were an apex for both $a$ and $a'$, then the
+coordinate of $t$ immediately following $s$ would equal both $a$ and $a'$.
+An apex cannot be a core vertex of $K_s$, since its node properly extends
+$s$.  Sending each base vertex $x$ to $(s,x)$ and its private inflation
+vertex to the corresponding apex therefore gives $D_s^+\cong K_s$.
+The number of edges of $D_s$ is exactly the number of edges of $K_s$.
+
+\proofpart{Intersections of fibres}
+If $(t,z)$ belongs to both $K_s$ and $K_u$, then $s,u$ are prefixes of $t$
+and hence are comparable.  Suppose $s\subsetneq u$.  A common point is
+necessarily an apex of $K_s$, and the base of its edge is the coordinate
+$u(\ell(s))$.  All common points would consequently be apices for this one
+base.  There is at most one selected edge with that base and one apex of
+that edge.  Hence distinct fibres meet in at most one point.
+
+\proofpart{The support forest}
+Let $Q$ be the bipartite graph between active base nodes and points
+belonging to at least two fibres, with adjacency given by membership.
+Suppose $Q$ contains a cycle and choose on it a base node $s$ of minimum
+length.  The two adjacent base nodes along the cycle are proper descendants
+of $s$.  Their two shared points with $K_s$ are distinct apices, so they
+come from distinct bases $a,a'$.  The adjacent nodes therefore lie in
+different immediate branches below $s$.
+
+On the remaining path of the cycle, consecutive base nodes are comparable.
+Every node on that path has length at least $\ell(s)$ and is different from
+$s$.  A node with these properties that is comparable with a proper
+descendant of $s$ is again a proper descendant of $s$: it either extends
+that descendant or is its prefix of length greater than $\ell(s)$.  Comparable
+proper descendants have the same coordinate immediately after $s$.
+Induction along the path therefore keeps all its nodes in a single branch
+below $s$, contradicting the two different branches at its endpoints.
+Thus $Q$ is a forest.
+
+\proofpart{Assembly}
+Root each component of $Q$ at a base node and order its base nodes by
+nondecreasing distance from the root.  For a nonroot node $s$, let $p_s$
+be its parent point.  If an earlier fibre $K_u$ meets $K_s$ at $p$, then
+$s-p-u$ is a path in $Q$.  The point $p$ cannot be a child of $s$, since
+every other base node adjacent to that child has greater depth.  Therefore
+$p=p_s$.  Each new fibre meets the preceding union in at most this one
+point, so the ordered fibres assemble by one-point amalgamations.
+Different components and the removed isolated vertices are combined by
+disjoint union.  The natural maps into $K$ identify exactly these common
+points; their union is an isomorphism onto $K$.
+\end{proof}
+
+The restriction to linear traces is essential here.  It is not imposed on
+the base host $H$, or on $\mathcal L(H)$ itself.
+
+\begin{lemma}[Inflation and assembly]
+\label{uniform-inflation-assembly}
+One-private-vertex inflation commutes, up to isomorphism, with finite
+disjoint unions and one-point amalgamations.  In particular, if $D$ is
+assembled from systems $J_i^{[k]}$, then $D^+$ is assembled from the
+corresponding systems $J_i^{[k+1]}$ in the same way.
+\end{lemma}
+\begin{proof}
+Index each new private vertex by its original edge.  The edge sets of
+one-point factors are disjoint because their edges have size at least two.
+After the factors are inflated, identify their selected old vertices and
+make no identification of new private vertices.  This gives exactly the
+inflation of their amalgamation.  The argument applies to any selected
+old vertex, including one that was private in an earlier expansion.
+Disjoint unions are immediate, and inflation leaves edgeless systems
+unchanged.
+\end{proof}
+
+For a graph $G$, define the iterated hosts
+\[
+ H_2(G)=G,\qquad H_j(G)=\mathcal L(H_{j-1}(G))\quad(j\ge3).
+\]
+
+\begin{proposition}[Iterated finite traces]
+\label{uniform-iterated-traces}
+For every $r\ge2$, each finite linear subhypergraph $K\subseteq H_r(G)$
+is assembled by disjoint unions and one-point amalgamations from
+$J^{[r]}$, with $J$ a finite subgraph of $G$, and finite edgeless systems.
+Every graph $J$ used in this construction has at most $|E(K)|$ edges.
+If $\chi(G)>\aleph_0$, then $\chi(H_r(G))>\aleph_0$.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+For $r=2$, take the finite graph $K$ itself, with the convention
+$J^{[2]}=J$.  At the next stage,
+Lemma~\ref{uniform-lift-trace} expresses $K$ as a forest assembly of
+$D_s^+$, where each $D_s$ is a finite linear trace in $H_{r-1}(G)$.
+Apply induction to $D_s$ and then Lemma~\ref{uniform-inflation-assembly}.
+This changes every constituent $J^{[r-1]}$ into $J^{[r]}$ and preserves
+the assembly.  Since $|E(D_s)|=|E(K_s)|\le|E(K)|$, the asserted bound on
+constituent edge counts also follows by induction.  The chromatic assertion
+is repeated application of Lemma~\ref{uniform-lift-chromatic}.
+\end{proof}
+
+\subsection{Reconstruction and obligatory systems}
+\label{uniform-reconstruction}
+
+\begin{lemma}[Intrinsic reconstruction]
+\label{uniform-intrinsic-equivalence}
+A finite reduced $r$-uniform hypergraph belongs to $\mathcal B_r$ if and
+only if it is linear, every hyperedge-node has at least $r-2$ incident
+Levi bridges, and every Berge cycle is even.
+\end{lemma}
+\begin{proof}
+For $J^{[r]}$ with $J$ bipartite, the $r-2$ private incidences at each
+hyperedge are bridges.  Its Berge cycles are exactly the graph cycles of
+$J$.  One-point amalgamation preserves linearity and bridges, and every
+Levi cycle of a one-point sum is contained in one factor.  These
+observations prove preservation of all three conditions.
+
+Conversely, let $L=I(F)$ and delete all bridges.  At each hyperedge-node
+there are at most two surviving incidences.  There cannot be exactly one,
+since any nonbridge incidence lies on a cycle, which uses a second
+nonbridge incidence at that node.  Thus the surviving degree is zero or
+two.  In a nontrivial remaining component $C$, suppress the hyperedge-nodes
+to obtain a graph $J_C$ on its point-nodes.  Linearity excludes parallel
+edges, and its cycles correspond to Berge cycles in $F$, so $J_C$ is
+simple and bipartite.
+
+Every hyperedge-node in $C$ has $r-2$ further point-neighbours, across
+bridges.  Each lies outside $C$, since a path inside $C$ would otherwise
+give an alternative route around that bridge.  No such neighbour can
+belong to two different hyperedges represented in $C$: an internal path
+between those hyperedge-nodes and their two incidences to the common
+point would again put bridges on a cycle.  The hyperedges represented in
+$C$, with all their vertices, therefore form exactly $J_C^{[r]}$.
+A hyperedge-node of surviving degree zero gives a single $r$-edge,
+which is $K_2^{[r]}$.
+
+Contract the bridge-deleted components.  The bridges give a simple forest
+$T$: a loop, parallel pair or quotient cycle would provide an alternative
+route around an original bridge.  A component is active if it contains a
+hyperedge-node; the inactive components are singleton point-nodes.  The
+pieces just constructed partition the original hyperedge set and cover
+all its vertices.
+
+For a point $p$, let $X(p)$ be its bridge-deleted component.  The pieces
+containing $p$ are the piece at $X(p)$ when this component is active,
+together with active neighbours whose connecting bridge has point
+endpoint $p$.  Root each tree at an active component and order active
+components by depth.  If the piece at $C$ shares $p$ with an earlier
+piece, then either $X(p)=C$, in which case the earlier neighbour is its
+parent, or $X(p)$ is the parent of $C$.  It cannot be a child, whose
+other neighbouring active components all have greater depth.  In both
+cases $p$ is the point endpoint of the unique parent edge of $C$.
+Consequently each piece meets the preceding union in at most one point.
+The running-intersection assembly from Proposition~5.2 now applies
+without change.  If $F$ has no edges, reducedness makes it empty, which
+is an edgeless generator.
+\end{proof}
+
+\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{uniform-classification}]
+Isolated vertices can be deleted and restored exactly as in Lemma~1.1.
+The same structural induction proves that this reduction preserves
+membership in $\mathcal B_r$.  We may therefore assume $F$ is reduced;
+the empty case is immediate.  Lemma~\ref{uniform-intrinsic-equivalence}
+establishes $(2)\Longleftrightarrow(3)$.
+
+For $(2)\Longrightarrow(1)$, Reiher's theorem makes every $J^{[r]}$ with
+$J$ finite bipartite obligatory \citep[Theorem~1.2]{reiher2024}.  The
+disjoint-union proof of Lemma~4.1 applies in any finite uniformity.  So do
+the rooted-abundance proof and the one-point amalgamation proof in
+Lemma~4.3 and Proposition~4.4: the bound
+$(m-1)(|V(F)|-1)$ on a hitting set for off-root copies does not depend on
+uniformity, and the auxiliary bounded-outdegree graph is unchanged.
+Thus obligatoriness is preserved by the operations defining
+$\mathcal B_r$.
+
+For $(1)\Longrightarrow(2)$, a nonlinear finite $r$-uniform hypergraph is
+not obligatory by the Erd\H{o}s--Hajnal--Rothschild nonlinearity
+obstruction \citep[Theorem~2]{ehr1973}; its all-uniformity scope is also
+recorded in \citet[Section~1]{reiher2024}.  It remains to treat linear
+$F\notin\mathcal B_r$.  Put $m=|E(F)|\ge1$.  By Theorem~7.1 choose a
+graph $G$ of uncountable chromatic number with no odd cycle of length at
+most $m$.  Proposition~\ref{uniform-iterated-traces} makes $H_r(G)$
+uncountably chromatic.  If $F$ embedded in this host, retain exactly the
+host hyperedges corresponding to its image.  They form a finite linear
+trace, which the proposition assembles from graph expansions $J^{[r]}$
+with $J\subseteq G$ and $|E(J)|\le m$.  Each such $J$ is bipartite:
+an odd cycle in it would have length at most $|E(J)|\le m$, contrary
+to the choice of $G$.  This would give $F\in\mathcal B_r$, a
+contradiction.  Hence $H_r(G)$ is an uncountably chromatic $F$-free host.
+\end{proof}
+
+The avoidance host depends on the finite edge bound $m$.  No claim is made
+that one uncountably chromatic host has only obligatory finite subgraphs;
+the trace assertion used above is restricted to linear subhypergraphs of
+the required finite size.
+
+\begin{corollary}[Canonical atoms in finite uniformity]
+\label{uniform-canonical-atoms}
+A finite reduced obligatory $r$-uniform hypergraph has a canonical
+partition of its hyperedges into single $r$-edges and systems $J^{[r]}$
+with $J$ finite, simple, bipartite and $2$-connected.  Distinct atoms meet
+in at most one point, and their incidence graph with shared points is a
+forest.  These atoms are precisely its one-point indecomposable
+edge-supported blocks.
+\end{corollary}
+\begin{proof}
+Every hyperedge-node has at most two nonbridge incidences.  In each cyclic
+block of the Levi graph it therefore has degree two, and it cannot
+belong to two different cyclic blocks.  Suppressing the hyperedge-nodes
+gives a simple $2$-connected bipartite graph $J$.  Its other $r-2$
+incidences per edge are bridges, with endpoints outside the block and
+pairwise distinct between its edges, by the same alternative-path
+argument as in Lemma~\ref{uniform-intrinsic-equivalence}.  Thus the
+edges of this block form $J^{[r]}$.  A hyperedge-node in no cyclic block
+has only bridge incidences and gives a single-edge atom.
+
+For completeness, the atom forest can be read directly from the
+block--cut forest of the Levi graph.  Assign each bridge block to the
+atom containing its hyperedge endpoint.  A cyclic-block atom consists
+of that block, its hyperedge-node cut vertices and the bridge blocks
+assigned to them; a single-edge atom consists of its hyperedge-node
+and its incident bridge blocks.  Each such set is connected in the
+block--cut forest, and the sets for different atoms are disjoint.
+Contract these connected sets.  The remaining nodes are point-nodes;
+discard those incident with only one atom.  The resulting graph is
+exactly the atom--shared-point incidence graph and is still a forest.
+In particular two atoms cannot share two points.  This construction
+also proves canonicity.  Finally, a component containing several atoms
+has a leaf atom that separates at its unique shared point.  Within an
+expansion of a $2$-connected graph, deleting a point-node from the Levi
+graph cannot separate its hyperedge-nodes into two nonempty classes:
+a private point is a leaf, and deleting a core point leaves the core
+connected.  This gives the claimed indecomposable blocks.
+\end{proof}
+
+\begin{remark}[The increased bridge requirement is strict]
+\label{uniform-four-edge-example}
+Consider the $4$-uniform hypergraph with five edges
+\[
+ \begin{aligned}
+ e&=\{a_1,a_2,a_3,p\},&
+ f&=\{b_1,b_2,b_3,q\},\\
+ g_i&=\{a_i,b_i,c_i,d_i\}\qquad&&(i=1,2,3),
+ \end{aligned}
+\]
+where all displayed vertices are distinct.  It is linear.  After its
+point leaves are removed, its Levi graph consists of three internally
+disjoint paths $e-a_i-g_i-b_i-f$, each of length four.  Every simple
+cycle uses two of these paths and hence has Levi length eight, or Berge
+length four.  Each edge has a bridge incidence, but $e$ and $f$ have
+exactly one, to $p$ and $q$ respectively.  The requirement of two bridge
+incidences in Theorem~\ref{uniform-classification} therefore excludes
+this system.  In particular, retaining the triple-system requirement
+of only one bridge would give a false higher-uniformity characterization.
+\end{remark}
+
+\subsection{Finite structure in higher uniformity}
+\label{uniform-finite-transfer}
+
+The classification in Theorem~\ref{uniform-classification} also transports
+the finite structure of Section~\ref{finite-parameter-consequences}.
+Fix an integer $r\ge3$.  As in the triple case, all systems in this
+subsection are finite, reduced, obligatory and have at least one edge.
+Their canonical atoms are single $r$-edges and the expansions $J^{[r]}$
+of finite $2$-connected simple bipartite graphs
+(Corollary~\ref{uniform-canonical-atoms}).  Put
+\[
+ n=|V(F)|,\qquad m=|E(F)|,\qquad
+ s=n-(r-2)m,\qquad \beta=(r-1)m-n+c,
+\]
+where $c$ is the number of connected components.  Thus $\beta$ is the
+cycle rank of the Levi graph, and $s$ is the order of a bipartite shadow.
+
+\begin{corollary}[Finite spectra at every uniformity]
+\label{uniform-finite-spectra}
+Let $m\ge1$, $1\le c\le m$, and $n$ be integers.  A reduced obligatory $r$-uniform
+hypergraph with $n$ vertices, $m$ edges and $c$ components exists if and
+only if
+\[
+ \boxed{
+ (r-2)m+2(c-1)+\left\lceil2\sqrt{m-c+1}\right\rceil
+ \le n\le (r-1)m+c.
+ }
+\]
+Every integer in this interval occurs.
+
+With the definitions of $s,\beta,c$ above, the canonical atom-count
+spectra and their feasibility conditions in
+Theorem~\ref{theorem-exact-canonical-atom-count-spectrum} and
+Proposition~\ref{proposition-componentwise-atom-count-spectrum} are
+unchanged, retaining the connectedness hypothesis of the former.
+For connected systems, the indecomposability phase diagram and its boundary
+descriptions in Theorem~\ref{theorem-indecomposability-phase-diagram}
+and Corollary~\ref{corollary-structural-boundary-rigidity} are likewise
+unchanged when $J^+$ is replaced by $J^{[r]}$ and a single triple by a
+single $r$-edge; the singleton exception is now $(m,n)=(1,r)$.
+
+Let $\mathcal D_r(F)$ denote the partitions of the original edge set into
+nonempty connected supported pieces that pairwise meet in at most one
+point and whose piece--shared-point incidence graph is a forest, ordered
+by refinement.  If $k$ is the number of canonical atoms and $\mu(p)$
+is their multiplicity at a shared point, then
+\[
+ \mathcal D_r(F)\cong\prod_p\Pi_{\mu(p)},
+ \qquad
+ \operatorname{rk}\mathcal D_r(F)=k-c.
+\]
+The exact lattice spectrum of
+Corollary~\ref{corollary-factorization-lattice-spectrum}, the cyclic
+budget of Proposition~\ref{proposition-cyclic-cost-budget}, and the
+piece-count bounds and equality cases of
+Corollary~\ref{corollary-sharp-piece-counts} hold with the same formulas
+and their stated hypotheses.  Every integer partition $\lambda\vdash k-c$
+is still realizable as an attachment excess profile with the actual atom
+list and component count fixed.
+\end{corollary}
+
+\begin{proof}
+For a singleton atom take its core to be $K_2$.  Write $v_i,e_i$ for the
+orders and sizes of all canonical cores.  The atom--point forest makes
+exactly $k-c$ vertex identifications, so
+\[
+ m=\sum_i e_i,\qquad
+ n=\sum_i\bigl(v_i+(r-2)e_i\bigr)-(k-c).
+\]
+Consequently
+\[
+ s=c+\sum_i(v_i-1),\qquad
+ \beta=\sum_i(e_i-v_i+1).
+\]
+Separately within each of the $c$ original components, take one-point sums
+of the ordinary cores, with bipartitions interchanged as needed.  Their
+disjoint union is a simple bipartite shadow with $s$ vertices, $m$ edges
+and $c$ components.  Conversely the expansion $J^{[r]}$ of any such
+shadow has exactly these parameters.  The ordinary bipartite-graph
+calculation in the proof of
+Theorem~\ref{theorem-order-size-component-spectrum} therefore gives the
+displayed interval and every integer realization.
+
+The two additive identities are precisely those used for the atom-count
+spectra and the cyclic budget.  Their bounds, the core constructions at
+each allowed rank and order, and the one-point assemblies that attain
+them remain available.  One-point indecomposability still means having
+one canonical atom.  The same minimum-degree and cut-vertex arguments
+on the ordinary cores thus give the phase diagram and boundary cases.
+
+For the lattice statements, a supported forest decomposition cannot
+split a canonical atom.  The local star replacement and disjoint
+component contractions in the proof of
+Lemma~\ref{lemma-local-decomposition-lattice-product} apply to the
+canonical atom--point forest without reference to edge size.  They give
+the displayed product and its rank.  To realize a prescribed excess
+profile, use the chain-and-leaves construction of
+Lemma~\ref{lemma-capacity-safe-profile-realization}.  Each connector
+uses two distinct vertices, whereas every supplied atom has at least
+$r\ge3$ vertices.  The resulting shared points separate atom branches,
+so the supplied atoms remain the actual canonical atoms of the assembly.
+This construction preserves the displayed additive counts.  The
+profile recovery and the counting injections consequently have exactly
+the same domains, ranks and actual piece numbers as before, proving all
+remaining assertions.
+\end{proof}
+
+\section{Exact chromatic spectra and simultaneous avoidance}
+\label{exact-spectra-extension}
+
+We now prescribe the chromatic number of the avoiding hosts.  The
+sequence lift treats linear forbidden systems, while a labelled
+reservoir construction treats nonlinear ones.  Together they give
+the full uncountable avoidance spectrum and explain the distinction
+between individual and simultaneous avoidance.
+For a family $\mathcal F$ of finite hypergraphs, a host is
+$\mathcal F$-free if it contains no member of $\mathcal F$.
+
+\subsection{Exact chromatic number of the sequence lift}
+\label{exact-lift-subsection}
+
+For an infinite cardinal $\kappa$, let $\mathcal L_\kappa(H)$ denote
+the construction in \eqref{uniform-lift-edge}, with
+$T=E(H)^{<\kappa}$ in place of $E(H)^{<\omega_1}$.
+Thus an edge has a $k$-edge as its base at $s$ and one apex at a node
+$t\succeq s^\frown a$.  The rank-raising lift admits an exact
+calibration, including at singular heights.
+
+\begin{lemma}[Sharp chromatic cap]
+\label{exact-lift-cap}
+Let $\kappa$ be an infinite cardinal and let $H$ be a $k$-uniform
+hypergraph with nonempty vertex set, where $k\ge2$ is finite.  Then
+\[
+ \chi\bigl(\mathcal L_\kappa(H)\bigr)
+   =\min\{\chi(H),\kappa\}.
+\]
+\end{lemma}
+
+\begin{proof}
+A proper colouring of $H$ induces a proper colouring of the lift by
+giving $(s,x)$ the colour of $x$: the base of every lifted edge is
+already nonmonochromatic.  Independently, colouring $(s,x)$ by its
+ordinal length $\ell(s)<\kappa$ is proper, because an apex has
+strictly greater length than its base.  These give both upper bounds.
+If $H$ is edgeless, both sides of the claimed equality are one.
+
+For the lower bound, suppose that $\theta<\chi(H)$ and
+$\theta<\kappa$, and consider a colouring
+$c:E(H)^{<\kappa}\times V(H)\to\theta$.
+At every node $s$, its restriction to $\{s\}\times V(H)$ has a
+monochromatic $H$-edge $a_s$, of some colour $b_s$.
+Starting with $s_0=\varnothing$, recursively put
+$s_{\alpha+1}=s_\alpha^\frown a_{s_\alpha}$ and take unions at
+limit ordinals.  The node $s_\alpha$ has length $\alpha<\kappa$,
+so every stage needed here lies in the tree.
+Since $\theta<\kappa$, there are $\alpha<\beta<\kappa$ with
+$b_{s_\alpha}=b_{s_\beta}$.
+The base $a_{s_\alpha}$ at $s_\alpha$, together with any vertex of
+the selected monochromatic base at $s_\beta$ as apex, is a
+monochromatic edge of $\mathcal L_\kappa(H)$.
+Thus no such $\theta$ colours suffice.  The recursion uses ordinal
+lengths and unions and does not assume that $\kappa$ is regular.
+\end{proof}
+
+Write
+\[
+ H_{2,\kappa}(G)=G,\qquad
+ H_{j,\kappa}(G)=\mathcal L_\kappa(H_{j-1,\kappa}(G))
+ \quad(j\ge3).
+\]
+Lemma~\ref{uniform-lift-trace} and
+Proposition~\ref{uniform-iterated-traces} remain valid for these
+hosts.  Their finite-trace proofs use only the prefix order, the
+unique base and apex of a selected edge, and finitely many active
+nodes; the tree height plays no further role.  In particular, every
+finite linear trace $K\subseteq H_{r,\kappa}(G)$ is assembled from
+$J^{[r]}$ with $J\subseteq G$ finite and
+$|E(J)|\le|E(K)|$, together with finite edgeless systems.
+The lemma also gives
+\[
+ \chi(G)=\kappa
+ \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
+ \chi(H_{r,\kappa}(G))=\kappa.
+\]
+
+At a successor $\kappa=\mu^+$, the order of these iterated hosts
+has a bound independent of $r$.  Put $\rho=2^\mu$.
+For any finite-uniform $H$ with $|V(H)|\le\rho$ we have
+$|E(H)|\le\rho$, and hence
+\begin{equation}\label{exact-successor-lift-order}
+ |E(H)^{<\mu^+}|
+ \le\mu^+\cdot\rho^\mu
+ =\rho,
+ \qquad
+ |V(\mathcal L_{\mu^+}(H))|\le\rho.
+\end{equation}
+Here every ordinal below $\mu^+$ has cardinality at most $\mu$,
+and $(2^\mu)^\mu=2^\mu$.  Thus a graph of order $\mu^+$ starts
+a finite iteration all of whose hosts have order at most $2^\mu$.
+
+\subsection{Simultaneous avoidance of finite linear families}
+\label{exact-linear-family-subsection}
+
+We use the cardinal form of the classical graph input: for every
+uncountable cardinal $\kappa$ and positive integer $m$, there is a
+graph $G$ with
+\[
+ |V(G)|=\chi(G)=\kappa
+\]
+and no odd cycle of length at most $m$
+\citep[Theorem~7.4, p.~76]{erdoshajnal1966}; this exact statement is
+also recorded in \citet[Theorem~C, p.~428]{egh1975}.
+For an infinite cardinal $\kappa$, write
+$2^{<\kappa}=\sup_{\nu<\kappa}2^\nu$, where the supremum runs
+over cardinals $\nu$.
+
+\begin{theorem}[Simultaneous linear-family avoidance]
+\label{exact-linear-family-avoidance}
+Let $r\ge3$ be finite, and let $\mathcal F$ be a finite family of
+finite linear $r$-uniform hypergraphs.  The following are equivalent:
+\begin{enumerate}
+\item No member of $\mathcal F$ belongs to $\mathcal B_r$.
+\item For every uncountable cardinal $\kappa$, there is an
+      $\mathcal F$-free $r$-uniform hypergraph $H$ with
+      $\chi(H)=\kappa$ and $|V(H)|\le2^{<\kappa}$.
+\item There is an $\mathcal F$-free $r$-uniform hypergraph of
+      uncountable chromatic number.
+\end{enumerate}
+The hosts in this assertion are not required to be linear.
+\end{theorem}
+
+\begin{proof}
+The second assertion implies the third, and the third implies the
+first by Theorem~\ref{uniform-classification}.
+Suppose the first assertion holds.
+For each $F\in\mathcal F$, choose a connected component $C_F$
+that does not belong to $\mathcal B_r$.
+Such a component exists because membership in $\mathcal B_r$ is
+componentwise, either by its intrinsic criterion or by disjoint-union
+closure.  Each $C_F$ is linear and has at least one edge, since
+isolated vertices belong to $\mathcal B_r$.
+Put
+\[
+ m=\max\bigl(\{1\}\cup
+          \{|E(C_F)|:F\in\mathcal F\}\bigr).
+\]
+
+First let $\kappa=\mu^+$, where $\mu$ is infinite, and take an
+exact-$\kappa$ graph $G$ of order $\kappa$ with no odd cycle of
+length at most $m$.
+The host $H=H_{r,\kappa}(G)$ has chromatic number $\kappa$ and,
+by \eqref{exact-successor-lift-order}, order at most $2^\mu$.
+If some $C_F$ embedded in $H$, its image would be a finite linear
+trace assembled from $J^{[r]}$ with $J\subseteq G$ and
+$|E(J)|\le|E(C_F)|\le m$.
+Each such $J$ is bipartite: a nonbipartite $J$ would contain an odd
+cycle of length at most $|E(J)|\le m$.
+The assembly would therefore put $C_F$ in $\mathcal B_r$, a
+contradiction.  Thus $H$ avoids every $C_F$ and hence every $F$.
+Since $2^{<\mu^+}=2^\mu$, this proves the required bound at
+successors.
+
+If $\kappa$ is an uncountable limit cardinal, choose uncountable
+successors $\kappa_j=\mu_j^+$, for $j<\operatorname{cf}(\kappa)$,
+cofinal in $\kappa$.
+For each $j$ use the preceding construction with the same components
+$C_F$, and take the disjoint union of the resulting hosts $H_j$.
+Every $C_F$ is connected, so an embedding of it into this union
+would lie in one summand.  The union therefore remains $C_F$-free
+for every $F$, and is consequently $\mathcal F$-free.
+Its chromatic number is $\sup_j\kappa_j=\kappa$, and its order
+is at most
+\[
+ \operatorname{cf}(\kappa)\cdot\sup_j2^{\mu_j}
+ \le 2^{<\kappa}.
+\]
+The last inequality uses
+$2^{<\kappa}\ge\sup_{\nu<\kappa}\nu^+=\kappa$ at a limit
+cardinal.  Passing first to connected forbidden components is what
+makes this disjoint-union step valid even when the original members
+of $\mathcal F$ are disconnected.
+\end{proof}
+
+\subsection{Exact calibration with bounded intersections}
+\label{exact-calibration}
+
+We use the simultaneous-colour property of Erd\H{o}s--Galvin--Hajnal
+\citep[Definition~6.2, p.~448]{egh1975}.  An edge labelling
+$\ell:E(S)\to I$ has property $P(S,I,\kappa)$ if every colouring
+$c:V(S)\to\theta$ with $0<\theta<\kappa$ has one colour $a<\theta$
+such that every label in $I$ occurs on an edge contained in
+$c^{-1}\{a\}$.  For nonempty $I$, this implies $\chi(S)\ge\kappa$.
+The following lemma extends Li's reservoir calibration for triple
+systems \citep[Section~6]{li2026}: it adds several old vertices to
+each base edge while retaining an explicit proper colouring.
+
+\begin{lemma}[Reservoir calibration]
+\label{exact-reservoir-step}
+Let $\mu$ be infinite, put $\kappa=\mu^+$, and fix a label set $I$
+of cardinality $2^\mu$.  Suppose $\sigma\ge2^\mu$ and
+$\operatorname{cf}(\sigma)>\mu$.  Let $k\ge2$ and $1\le d\le i$
+be finite integers.  Suppose that $S$ is a simple $k$-uniform
+hypergraph on at most $\sigma$ vertices, distinct edges of $S$
+meet in at most $i$ vertices, and $P(S,I,\kappa)$ holds.
+Then there is a simple $(k+d)$-uniform hypergraph $L$ such that
+distinct edges meet in at most $i$ vertices and
+\[
+ |V(L)|\le2^\sigma,\qquad \chi(L)=\kappa.
+\]
+\end{lemma}
+
+\begin{proof}
+Put $R=\sigma^+$ and $\Lambda=2^\sigma$.  Then $R\le\Lambda$ and
+$\Lambda^\sigma=\Lambda$.  Fix $q:R\to\kappa$ whose fibres are
+cofinal in $R$; a partition of $R$ into $\kappa$ sets of cardinality
+$R$ supplies such a map.  Take pairwise disjoint levels
+$(V_\alpha)_{\alpha<R}$ of cardinality $\Lambda$.
+Write $V_{<\alpha}=\bigcup_{\beta<\alpha}V_\beta$, and let
+$\operatorname{rk}(x)=\alpha$ for $x\in V_\alpha$.
+
+An admissible reservoir at level $\alpha$ is a family
+$\mathbf X=(X_p)_{p\in I}$ of pairwise disjoint subsets of
+$V_{<\alpha}$, each empty or of cardinality $\sigma$, such that
+$q(\operatorname{rk}(x))\ne q(\alpha)$ whenever $x\in\bigcup_pX_p$.
+There are at most $\Lambda$ such families at each level: each
+coordinate has at most $\Lambda^\sigma=\Lambda$ possibilities,
+and $|I|\le\sigma$.
+For every admissible $\mathbf X$, install $\sigma$ labelled copies
+of $S$ in $V_\alpha$, with all installed copies at that level
+mutually vertex-disjoint.  This requires at most
+$\Lambda\cdot\sigma\cdot\sigma=\Lambda$ vertices.
+
+In a copy $B$ installed for $\mathbf X$, call $e\in E(B)$ active
+when $X_{\ell(e)}\ne\varnothing$.  Choose $d$-element sets
+$A_B(e)\subseteq X_{\ell(e)}$ for the active edges, pairwise
+disjoint within $B$.  These choices are possible because
+$|E(S)|\le\sigma$, $d$ is finite, and every nonempty reservoir
+has cardinality $\sigma$.  Put $V(L)=\bigcup_{\alpha<R}V_\alpha$
+and take as edges precisely the sets
+\[
+ e\cup A_B(e)\qquad(e\text{ active in an installed copy }B).
+\]
+All choices are fixed before considering a vertex colouring.
+
+The highest-level vertices of an edge recover its base edge and
+its installed copy, so $L$ is simple and $(k+d)$-uniform.
+For two edges in the same copy, the old vertex sets are disjoint,
+so their intersection has size at most $i$.  Edges from different
+copies at one level have disjoint bases and can intersect only
+in their old vertex sets, of size $d$.  For edges based at different
+levels, the intersection is contained in the $d$ old vertices of
+the higher edge.  Thus the intersection bound $i$ is preserved.
+The map $x\mapsto q(\operatorname{rk}(x))$ is a proper
+$\kappa$-colouring by reservoir admissibility, and
+$|V(L)|=R\Lambda=\Lambda$.
+
+To prove the lower bound, consider any $c:V(L)\to\theta$ with
+$0<\theta<\kappa$, and write $C_a=c^{-1}\{a\}$.
+Call a colour large when $|C_a|\ge\sigma$, and let $D$ be the
+union of the other colour classes.  There are at most $\mu$
+colours, so $\operatorname{cf}(\sigma)>\mu$ gives $|D|<\sigma$.
+At least one colour is large.
+For $\xi<\kappa$, let
+$Q_\xi=\bigcup\{V_\beta:q(\beta)=\xi\}$.
+A fixed large colour $a$ has at most one value $\xi$ for which
+$|C_a\setminus Q_\xi|<\sigma$: two such values would express
+$C_a$ as a union of two sets of cardinality below $\sigma$.
+Since there are at most $\mu$ large colours, choose
+$\xi<\mu^+$ exceptional for none of them.
+
+Assign distinct labels $p_a\in I$ to the large colours and choose
+$X_{p_a}\in[C_a\setminus Q_\xi]^\sigma$, putting all other
+$X_p=\varnothing$.  Their union has size $\sigma$, so its ranks
+are bounded in $R=\sigma^+$.  Choose
+$\alpha\in q^{-1}\{\xi\}$ above those ranks.
+This is an admissible reservoir at level $\alpha$.
+Fewer than $\sigma$ of its $\sigma$ disjoint installed copies
+meet $D$, since the copies meeting $D$ inject into $D$.
+Choose an installed copy $B$ avoiding $D$.
+
+By $P(S,I,\kappa)$ applied to $c|_B$, some colour $a$ contains
+a base edge of every label.  It is large because $B$ avoids $D$.
+In particular, there is a monochromatic base edge $e$ of label
+$p_a$.  It is active, and
+$A_B(e)\subseteq X_{p_a}\subseteq C_a$.
+Thus $e\cup A_B(e)$ is a monochromatic edge of $L$.
+No colouring with fewer than $\kappa$ colours is proper, proving
+$\chi(L)=\kappa$.
+\end{proof}
+
+Define $\beth_0(\mu)=\mu$ and
+$\beth_{j+1}(\mu)=2^{\beth_j(\mu)}$ for finite $j$.
+
+\begin{theorem}[Exact bounded-intersection calibration]
+\label{exact-bounded-calibration}
+Let $\mu$ be an infinite cardinal, $r\ge3$ finite, and
+$1\le i\le r-2$.  Put $t=\lceil(r-1)/i\rceil$.
+There is a simple $r$-uniform hypergraph $L$ whose distinct edges
+intersect in at most $i$ vertices and such that
+\[
+ \chi(L)=\mu^+,\qquad |V(L)|\le\beth_t(\mu).
+\]
+In particular, for $i=1$ there is a linear $r$-uniform hypergraph
+of chromatic number exactly $\mu^+$ on at most
+$\beth_{r-1}(\mu)$ vertices.
+\end{theorem}
+
+\begin{proof}
+Write $k=r-i$, $j=\min\{i,k-1\}$ and $m=t-1$.
+Then $k\ge2$, $m\ge1$, $1\le j<k$, and $k\le mj+1$.
+Indeed, if $i\le k-1$, then $j=i$ and
+$m=\lceil(k-1)/i\rceil$; otherwise $j=k-1$ and $m=1$.
+
+Erd\H{o}s--Galvin--Hajnal's Corollary~12.3
+\citep[p.~485]{egh1975} gives a $(k,j)$-system $S$ of size
+$\sigma=\beth_m(\mu)$ with
+$P^*(S,\sigma,\mu^+,j)$.
+Taking $V(S)=\bigcup E(S)$ gives $|V(S)|\le\sigma$.
+Here distinct edges of a $(k,j)$-system intersect in at most $j$
+vertices, and their stronger property $P^*$ implies $P$.
+Coarsen the $\sigma$ labels by a surjection onto a set $I$ of
+cardinality $2^\mu\le\sigma$; the property $P$ is preserved.
+Since $m\ge1$, K\"onig's theorem gives
+\[
+ \operatorname{cf}(\sigma)
+ =\operatorname{cf}(2^{\beth_{m-1}(\mu)})
+ >\beth_{m-1}(\mu)\ge\mu.
+\]
+Apply Lemma~\ref{exact-reservoir-step} with $d=i$.
+The output has rank $k+i=r$, intersection bound $i$, chromatic
+number exactly $\mu^+$, and order at most
+$2^\sigma=\beth_t(\mu)$.
+\end{proof}
+
+\begin{corollary}[All uncountable chromatic cardinals]
+\label{exact-all-cardinals}
+For every finite $r\ge3$, every $1\le i\le r-2$, and every
+uncountable cardinal $\kappa$, there is a simple $r$-uniform
+hypergraph of chromatic number exactly $\kappa$ whose distinct
+edges intersect in at most $i$ vertices.  If $\kappa$ is a limit
+cardinal and $t=\lceil(r-1)/i\rceil$, the host can be chosen
+with order at most $\sup_{\aleph_0\le\mu<\kappa}\beth_t(\mu)$.
+In particular, its order is exactly $\kappa$ when $\kappa$ is
+an uncountable strong limit cardinal.
+\end{corollary}
+
+\begin{proof}
+The successor case is Theorem~\ref{exact-bounded-calibration}.
+For a limit cardinal $\kappa$, take a cofinal family of uncountable
+successor cardinals $\kappa_\eta<\kappa$ and form a disjoint union
+of the corresponding hosts.  The intersection bound is preserved,
+and the chromatic number of the union is
+$\sup_\eta\kappa_\eta=\kappa$.
+Put $M=\sup_{\aleph_0\le\mu<\kappa}\beth_t(\mu)$.  Since
+$M\ge\kappa$, the union has order at most
+$\operatorname{cf}(\kappa)\cdot M=M$.  At a strong limit
+$\kappa$, every finite iterate $\beth_t(\mu)$ with $\mu<\kappa$
+is below $\kappa$, so $M=\kappa$; the reverse order bound
+follows from $\chi(H)\le|V(H)|$.
+This argument includes singular cardinals.
+\end{proof}
+
+\begin{remark}[Classical size benchmark]
+Conditional optimality under GCH already follows from
+Erd\H{o}s--Galvin--Hajnal.  Their Corollary~2.2
+\citep[p.~433]{egh1975} implies that an $(r,i)$-system on at most
+$\mu^{+m}$ vertices is $\mu$-colourable when $r\ge mi+2$.
+Taking $m=t-1$ gives the ZFC lower bound
+$|V(H)|\ge\mu^{+t}$ whenever $\chi(H)>\mu$.
+Under GCH, their Corollary~12.3, applied directly with rank $r$
+and parameter $t$, supplies such a host on $\mu^{+t}$ vertices.
+Applying Corollary~2.2 again with base $\mu^+$ and parameter
+$t-1$ gives chromatic number at most $\mu^+$, hence exactly
+$\mu^+$.  Thus the exact GCH threshold is a classical consequence.
+The construction above combines their simultaneous-colour bases
+with Li's reservoir method to retain exact chromatic calibration
+in ZFC.  At the remaining endpoint $i=r-1$, the complete
+$r$-uniform hypergraph on $\mu^+$ vertices already has order
+and chromatic number $\mu^+$.
+\end{remark}
+
+\subsection{The uncountable avoidance spectrum}
+\label{exact-spectrum-subsection}
+
+For a finite $r$-uniform hypergraph $F$, define its uncountable
+avoidance spectrum to be the class
+\[
+ \operatorname{Spec}^{\mathrm{unc}}_r(F)=
+ \{\kappa>\aleph_0:
+      \text{some $F$-free $r$-uniform $H$ has $\chi(H)=\kappa$}\},
+\]
+where $\kappa$ ranges over cardinals.
+For a nonlinear $F$, put
+\[
+ \tau(F)=\max\{|e\cap f|:e,f\in E(F),\ e\ne f\}.
+\]
+Thus $2\le\tau(F)\le r-1$.
+
+\begin{theorem}[Exact uncountable avoidance spectra]
+\label{exact-avoidance-spectrum}
+For every finite $r\ge3$ and finite $r$-uniform hypergraph $F$,
+\[
+ \operatorname{Spec}^{\mathrm{unc}}_r(F)=
+ \begin{cases}
+  \varnothing,&F\in\mathcal B_r,\\[2pt]
+  \{\kappa:\kappa>\aleph_0\text{ is a cardinal}\},
+       &F\notin\mathcal B_r.
+ \end{cases}
+\]
+If $F\notin\mathcal B_r$ and $\kappa=\mu^+$ with $\mu$ infinite,
+an $F$-free host of chromatic number $\kappa$ can be chosen with
+\[
+ |V(H)|\le
+ \begin{cases}
+  2^\mu,&F\text{ is linear},\\[2pt]
+  \beth_{\lceil(r-1)/(\tau(F)-1)\rceil}(\mu),
+       &F\text{ is nonlinear}.
+ \end{cases}
+\]
+For linear $F\notin\mathcal B_r$, the stronger bound
+$|V(H)|\le2^{<\kappa}$ holds at every uncountable $\kappa$.
+\end{theorem}
+
+\begin{proof}
+If $F\in\mathcal B_r$, the classification theorem makes $F$
+obligatory, so the spectrum is empty.
+If $F$ is linear and does not belong to $\mathcal B_r$, apply
+Theorem~\ref{exact-linear-family-avoidance} to the singleton family
+$\{F\}$.
+
+Suppose instead that $F$ is nonlinear, and set $i=\tau(F)-1$.
+Then $1\le i\le r-2$.
+For $\kappa=\mu^+$, Theorem~\ref{exact-bounded-calibration}
+supplies an $r$-uniform host of chromatic number $\kappa$, of order
+at most $\beth_{\lceil(r-1)/i\rceil}(\mu)$, in which distinct
+edges intersect in at most $i$ points.
+This host is $F$-free: an embedding of $F$ would preserve the
+$\tau(F)=i+1$ shared vertices of a pair of its edges.
+This proves both the successor assertion and its stated order bound.
+
+For an uncountable limit $\kappa$, choose cofinal uncountable
+successors and take a disjoint union of the corresponding hosts
+with the same intersection bound $i$.
+The union still has pairwise edge intersections at most $i$ and
+therefore omits $F$; its chromatic number is the supremum of the
+successor chromatic numbers, namely $\kappa$.
+This completes all cases, including singular cardinals.
+\end{proof}
+
+The triple-system spectrum is established in
+\citet[Theorem~7.1]{li2026}.
+Here the classification in every uniformity and the bounded-intersection
+calibration extend the dichotomy to all finite $r\ge3$.
+The quantitative bounds are witness bounds; no minimum order is
+asserted for an individual forbidden hypergraph.
+
+\subsection{A finite-family obstruction in every uniformity}
+\label{exact-mixed-family-obstruction-section}
+
+The assumption that every forbidden member is linear in the simultaneous
+avoidance result cannot be dropped.  The following construction extends
+the triple-system example used by \citet[Section~7]{li2026} in connection
+with Erd\H{o}s Problem~\#1177.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Individually avoidable, jointly obligatory]
+\label{exact-mixed-family-obstruction}
+For every finite $r\ge3$ there is a finite family $\mathcal F_r$ of
+finite simple $r$-uniform hypergraphs such that no member of
+$\mathcal F_r$ is obligatory, but every $\mathcal F_r$-free
+$r$-uniform hypergraph has countable chromatic number.
+\end{proposition}
+
+\begin{proof}
+For $2\le t\le r-1$, let $N_{r,t}$ consist of two $r$-edges whose
+intersection has size $t$, with no isolated vertices, and put
+\[
+ \mathcal N_r=\{N_{r,t}:2\le t\le r-1\}.
+\]
+A simple $r$-uniform host avoids $\mathcal N_r$ precisely when it is
+linear.  Every member of $\mathcal N_r$ is non-obligatory, by the
+classical nonlinear obstruction \citep{ehr1973}.
+
+Let $C_7^{[3]}$ be the private triple expansion of the $7$-cycle.
+Define $\mathcal E_r$ to contain one representative of each isomorphism
+type of a reduced linear $r$-uniform hypergraph $E$ with exactly seven
+edges $e_1,\ldots,e_7$ such that there are triples
+$a_j\subseteq e_j$ for which the triple system
+$\{a_1,\ldots,a_7\}$ is isomorphic to $C_7^{[3]}$.
+This is a finite family: every such $E$ has at most $7r$ vertices.
+It is nonempty, since $C_7^{[r]}$ is a member.
+Every $E\in\mathcal E_r$ retains the seven distinct cycle vertices
+and seven distinct edges of the displayed loose triple cycle, so it
+contains a Berge cycle of odd length seven.  Hence no member of
+$\mathcal E_r$ belongs to $\mathcal B_r$, and the classification
+theorem shows that each is non-obligatory.
+
+Set $\mathcal F_r=\mathcal N_r\cup\mathcal E_r$, and suppose that
+an $r$-uniform host $H$ has uncountable chromatic number.
+If $H$ is nonlinear, it contains a member of $\mathcal N_r$.
+If $H$ is linear, choose a triple $a_e\subseteq e$ from every edge
+$e\in E(H)$, and let $H_3$ be the resulting triple system on $V(H)$.
+The chosen triples are distinct and form a linear triple system,
+because the original edges meet pairwise in at most one point.
+Every proper colouring of $H_3$ is a proper colouring of $H$, so
+\[
+ \chi(H_3)\ge\chi(H)>\aleph_0.
+\]
+The theorem of Hajnal--Komj\'ath that $C_7^{[3]}$ is linearly
+obligatory \citep{hajnalkomjath2008} supplies a copy of that triple
+system in $H_3$.  Restore the seven original $r$-edges from which
+its triples were selected, and take their union as vertex set.
+The resulting reduced seven-edge subhypergraph of $H$ is a member
+of $\mathcal E_r$ up to isomorphism.  Thus every uncountably
+chromatic host contains a member of $\mathcal F_r$, as required.
+\end{proof}
+
+For $r=3$, the family $\mathcal E_3$ consists only of
+$C_7^{[3]}$, and $\mathcal N_3$ consists only of two triples meeting
+in two points.  For larger $r$, the definition of $\mathcal E_r$
+allows all identifications among the added points that preserve
+linearity.  This finite completion family is needed because choosing
+triples from a linear $r$-uniform host does not force the restored
+edges to have globally private added vertices.
+
+\section{Obligatory atoms and Sidorenko expansions}
+\label{density-extension}
+
+The canonical description also connects obligatoriness with a finite
+counting question.  A bipartite private expansion can be forced by
+uncountable chromatic number while having fewer copies than the random
+density benchmark in a finite host.  We establish this for a single
+canonical atom in every uniformity, using the specific graph and
+rank-layer construction of OpenAI \citep{openai161}.
+
+The conclusions about that graph in this section are conditional on
+the rank-layer limit in the pinned version of the preprint.  We give
+the transfer proofs, including an additional proper-subgraph limit
+extracted from its coordinate expansion.  The preprint's finite-field
+estimate is an external input.  The classification and chromatic
+spectra in the preceding sections do not use it.
+
+The distinction is also relevant to a published conjecture of
+Nie--Spiro \citep[Conjecture~5.1]{niespiro2025}, which asks whether
+every bipartite graph has a Sidorenko private expansion in some
+uniformity.  The same fixed graph below has no such expansion.
+The transfer requires constructing an actual symmetric hypergraph
+kernel; a graph density counterexample alone does not provide one.
+We begin with a universal marginal-realization theorem and
+quantitative density bounds that are independent of the external
+rank-layer input.  They describe both the available realizations
+and the size of possible density errors as the uniformity grows.
+
+\subsection{Universal pair marginals and matching symmetrization}
+
+All probability spaces below are finite and all their atoms have
+positive mass.  For a symmetric nonnegative $r$-variable kernel $U$
+and a finite simple $r$-uniform hypergraph $F$, put
+\[
+ t(F,U)=\mathbb E\prod_{e\in E(F)}U((X_v)_{v\in e}),
+\]
+where the $X_v$ are independent samples.  Symmetry makes the ordering
+within an edge immaterial.  Write $p(U)=\mathbb E U$.
+For a finite simple $r$-uniform host $G$ of order $n>0$, use
+\[
+ t(F,G)=\frac{\operatorname{Hom}(F,G)}{n^{|V(F)|}},
+ \qquad p_r(G)=\frac{r!\,|E(G)|}{n^r}.
+\]
+Thus all edge-preserving maps are counted, including noninjective maps.
+We call $F$ \emph{Sidorenko} if
+$t(F,G)\ge p_r(G)^{|E(F)|}$ for every such host.  This is the
+ordinary hypergraph homomorphism-density convention of
+\citet{conlonleesidorenko2024} and \citet{niespiro2025}.
+For $n\ge r$, the normalization $p_r(G)$ differs from
+$|E(G)|/\binom nr$ by the
+factor $(n)_r/n^r$, which tends to one as $n$ tends to infinity.
+
+For a simple graph $J$, let $J^{[r]}$ be its private $r$-uniform
+expansion, with $r-2$ distinct new vertices on each graph edge.
+The convention at rank two is $J^{[2]}=J$.
+The two-variable marginal of $U$ is
+\[
+ M_U(x,y)=\mathbb E_{Z_3,\ldots,Z_r}U(x,y,Z_3,\ldots,Z_r).
+\]
+The private vertices occur in exactly one edge, so Fubini's theorem gives
+\begin{equation}\label{density-expansion-identity}
+ t(J^{[r]},U)=t(J,M_U),\qquad p(U)=\mathbb E M_U.
+\end{equation}
+An arbitrary graph kernel need not be such a marginal.  We first
+characterize which diluted pair marginals can always be realized;
+the symmetric construction needed for expansions follows as a
+special case.
+
+For $r\ge2$, write $\mathfrak M_r$ for the convex hull in
+$\mathbb R^{\binom r2}$ of the incidence vectors of all matchings
+of $K_r$, including the empty matching.  Edmonds' matching-polytope
+theorem \citep[Section~2, Theorem~(P)]{edmonds1965matching} gives its description
+\begin{equation}\label{density-matching-inequalities}
+ \lambda_{ij}\ge0,\qquad
+ \sum_{j\ne i}\lambda_{ij}\le1,\qquad
+ \sum_{\{i,j\}\subseteq S}\lambda_{ij}\le\frac{|S|-1}{2}
+ \quad (|S|\text{ odd}).
+\end{equation}
+The last family may be restricted to $|S|\ge3$.
+The following identifies this polytope as the exact region of
+coefficients that realize every collection of finite pair kernels.
+In this general statement the spaces may differ, and the joint
+density need not be symmetric.  We write $\lambda_{ji}=\lambda_{ij}$
+when an unordered pair is indexed in the opposite order.
+
+\begin{theorem}[Universal pair-marginal profiles]
+\label{density-universal-profiles}
+Let $r\ge2$ and $\lambda\in\mathbb R^{\binom r2}$.  The following
+are equivalent.
+\begin{enumerate}
+\item For every collection of finite probability spaces
+$(\Omega_i,\mu_i)$ and nonnegative kernels
+$V_{ij}:\Omega_i\times\Omega_j\to\mathbb R$ satisfying
+\[
+ \mathbb E_{x_j}V_{ij}(x_i,x_j)=1,
+ \qquad \mathbb E_{x_i}V_{ij}(x_i,x_j)=1,
+\]
+there is a nonnegative density $U$ of mean one on
+$\prod_{i=1}^r\Omega_i$ whose $(i,j)$-marginal is
+\[
+ M_{ij}=1+\lambda_{ij}(V_{ij}-1)
+ \qquad(1\le i<j\le r).
+\]
+\item The preceding assertion holds when all spaces are the same
+finite probability space and all $V_{ij}$ are the same symmetric
+nonnegative kernel $V$ with constant row integral one.
+\item $\lambda\in\mathfrak M_r$.
+\end{enumerate}
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+The first assertion implies the second.  For the third implying the
+first, write $\lambda=\sum_M w_M\mathbf1_M$, where $w_M\ge0$ and
+$\sum_Mw_M=1$, and set
+\[
+ U(x_1,\ldots,x_r)
+   =\sum_Mw_M\prod_{\{i,j\}\in M}V_{ij}(x_i,x_j).
+\]
+Each product has mean one because its factors use disjoint variables.
+Its $(i,j)$-marginal is $V_{ij}$ if $\{i,j\}\in M$ and is one
+otherwise: in the latter case every factor touching $i$ or $j$ has
+its other variable integrated out.  This proves the required formula.
+
+It remains to prove the inequalities
+\eqref{density-matching-inequalities} from the second assertion.
+For any even $N$, take the uniform probability space $[N]$, a
+fixed-point-free involution $f$ of $[N]$, and
+\[
+ V_N(x,y)=N\mathbf1_{\{y=f(x)\}}.
+\]
+This is a symmetric nonnegative kernel with row integral one.
+Nonnegativity of each prescribed pair marginal, both on and off
+the graph of $f$, gives
+$1+\lambda_{ij}(N-1)\ge0$ and $1-\lambda_{ij}\ge0$.
+Letting even $N$ tend to infinity yields $0\le\lambda_{ij}\le1$.
+
+Give $(Z_1,\ldots,Z_r)$ the joint probability law with density $U$
+relative to the uniform product measure.  Fix $i$ and put
+$A_j=\{Z_j=f(Z_i)\}$ for $j\ne i$.  The specified pair marginals
+give
+\[
+ \mathbb P(A_j)=\lambda_{ij}+\frac{1-\lambda_{ij}}N.
+\]
+For distinct $j,k\ne i$, the event $A_j\cap A_k$ implies
+$Z_j=Z_k$.  Since $f$ has no fixed point, the $(j,k)$-marginal
+therefore gives
+\[
+ \mathbb P(A_j\cap A_k)
+ \le\mathbb P(Z_j=Z_k)=\frac{1-\lambda_{jk}}N\le\frac1N.
+\]
+For any finite family of events,
+$\sum_j\mathbf1_{A_j}\le
+1+\sum_{j<k}\mathbf1_{A_j\cap A_k}$ pointwise.  Consequently
+\[
+ \sum_{j\ne i}\left(\lambda_{ij}
+           +\frac{1-\lambda_{ij}}N\right)
+ \le1+\frac{\binom{r-1}{2}}N.
+\]
+Letting $N$ tend to infinity proves the degree inequalities.
+
+For the odd-set inequalities use the uniform space $\{-1,1\}$
+and $V(a,b)=1-ab$.  The prescribed pair marginals imply
+$\mathbb E Z_iZ_j=-\lambda_{ij}$.  For every odd subset $S$,
+the integer $\sum_{i\in S}Z_i$ is odd, and thus
+\[
+ 1\le\mathbb E\left(\sum_{i\in S}Z_i\right)^2
+   =|S|-2\sum_{\{i,j\}\subseteq S}\lambda_{ij}.
+\]
+These are precisely the remaining inequalities.  Edmonds' theorem
+now gives $\lambda\in\mathfrak M_r$.
+\end{proof}
+
+
+\begin{lemma}[Matching symmetrization]\label{density-matching}
+Let $V$ be a finite symmetric nonnegative kernel with
+$\mathbb E_y V(x,y)=1$ for every $x$.  For an integer $r\ge2$, let
+$\mathcal M_r$ be the set of matchings of size $\lfloor r/2\rfloor$
+in the complete graph on $[r]$, and define
+\[
+ U_r(x_1,\ldots,x_r)=
+ \frac{1}{|\mathcal M_r|}
+ \sum_{M\in\mathcal M_r}\prod_{\{a,b\}\in M}V(x_a,x_b).
+\]
+Then $U_r$ is symmetric and nonnegative, has mean one, and
+\[
+ M_{U_r}=1+\lambda_r(V-1),\qquad
+ \lambda_r=\frac{2\lfloor r/2\rfloor}{r(r-1)}
+ =\begin{cases}(r-1)^{-1},&r\text{ even},\\ r^{-1},&r\text{ odd}.
+ \end{cases}
+\]
+\end{lemma}
+\begin{proof}
+Each matching product has mean one, because its factors have disjoint
+variable sets.  In its two-variable marginal, a matching containing
+$\{1,2\}$ contributes $V(x_1,x_2)$.  Every other matching contributes
+one: any partner of vertex $1$ or $2$ is integrated out using the
+constant row integral, and the remaining disjoint pairs have mean one.
+The proportion of matchings containing a given pair is their common
+number of edges divided by $\binom r2$, namely $\lambda_r$.
+Permutation invariance of the set of matchings proves symmetry.
+\end{proof}
+
+The coefficient in Lemma~\ref{density-matching} is optimal in the
+following universal sense: the assertion that every finite symmetric
+nonnegative kernel $V$ with row integral one admits a symmetric
+nonnegative mean-one $r$-kernel with pair marginal $1+\lambda(V-1)$ holds
+precisely for $0\le\lambda\le\lambda_r$.
+Indeed, Theorem~\ref{density-universal-profiles} gives
+$(r-1)\lambda\le1$ when $r$ is even and
+$\binom r2\lambda\le(r-1)/2$ when $r$ is odd; its nonnegativity
+condition excludes negative coefficients.  The lemma attains the
+endpoint, and mixing its kernel with the constant one attains the
+remaining interval.
+The scalar parity obstruction is the familiar minimum-correlation
+bound for exchangeable signs \citep{hoernig2018}.
+Products averaged over perfect or near-perfect matchings also occur
+in the quantum marginal construction of
+\citet[Lemma~4.4]{allerstorfer2026}.
+Theorem~\ref{density-universal-profiles} addresses the full array of
+coefficients for arbitrary finite classical pair kernels.
+
+
+\subsection{Uniform density estimates as uniformity increases}
+
+For bounded symmetric kernels, increasing the uniformity constrains
+the pair marginal.  We use the first two terms of the classical
+Hoeffding orthogonal decomposition \citep{hoeffding1948} and give the
+short projection argument in full.
+Nie--Spiro's exponent estimate
+\citep[Theorem~1.13]{niespiro2025} already yields one-sided
+approximate Sidorenko bounds for expansions at fixed positive
+density.  The results below give explicit two-sided estimates,
+uniform over bounded kernels, together with optimal rates and a
+girth-sensitive refinement under regularity.
+All $L^2$ norms refer to the relevant product probability measure.
+
+\begin{lemma}[Two-coordinate projection bound]
+\label{density-projection-bound}
+Let $r\ge2$ and let $U:\Omega^r\to[0,1]$ be symmetric.  Set
+$p=\mathbb EU$, $d(x)=\mathbb E[U\mid X_1=x]$, and
+$M(x,y)=\mathbb E[U\mid X_1=x,X_2=y]$.  With
+\[
+ h=d-p,\qquad k(x,y)=M(x,y)-p-h(x)-h(y),
+\]
+one has
+\begin{equation}\label{density-hoeffding}
+ r\|h\|_2^2+\binom r2\|k\|_2^2\le p(1-p),
+ \qquad
+ \|M-p\|_2^2\le\frac{2p(1-p)}r.
+\end{equation}
+If $d\equiv p$, the second bound improves to
+\begin{equation}\label{density-regular-projection}
+ \|M-p\|_2^2\le\frac{2p(1-p)}{r(r-1)}.
+\end{equation}
+\end{lemma}
+\begin{proof}
+The mean of $h$ is zero and both one-variable integrals of $k$
+vanish.  Hence the random variables $h(X_i)$ and $k(X_i,X_j)$,
+over all singletons and pairs, are mutually orthogonal in $L^2$.
+Their inner products with $U-p$ are respectively $\|h\|_2^2$
+and $\|k\|_2^2$, by conditional expectation.  Subtracting their
+sum from $U-p$ leaves an orthogonal remainder.  Thus their squared
+norms sum to at most $\operatorname{Var}U\le p(1-p)$, proving
+the first bound.  Furthermore
+\[
+ \|M-p\|_2^2=2\|h\|_2^2+\|k\|_2^2
+ \le\frac2r\left(r\|h\|_2^2+\binom r2\|k\|_2^2\right),
+\]
+because $r\ge2$.  If $h=0$, use the first bound directly.
+\end{proof}
+
+\begin{theorem}[Two-sided density approximation]
+\label{density-uniform-approximation}
+Let $F$ be a finite simple graph with $m\ge1$ edges and $r\ge2$.
+Every symmetric $[0,1]$-valued $r$-kernel $U$ of mean $p$ satisfies
+\begin{equation}\label{density-density-error}
+ \left|t(F^{[r]},U)-p^m\right|
+    \le\frac{m(m-1)}r\,p(1-p).
+\end{equation}
+The same inequality holds for every finite simple $r$-uniform host
+$G$, with $p=p_r(G)$ and homomorphism-density normalization.
+The order $r^{-1}$ cannot be improved uniformly, even for $F=P_3$
+and $p=1/2$.
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+Write $D=M_U-p$ and $f(s)=t(F,p+sD)$ for $0\le s\le1$.
+The private-expansion identity gives $f(1)=t(F^{[r]},U)$.
+We have $f(0)=p^m$ and $f'(0)=0$, since $\mathbb ED=0$.
+Each of the $m(m-1)$ terms in $f''(s)$ has two factors $D$
+and $m-2$ factors in $[0,1]$.  Cauchy--Schwarz on the entire
+product probability space bounds its absolute value by
+$\|D\|_2^2$, whether the two corresponding graph edges meet or
+are disjoint.  Taylor's formula with integral remainder yields
+\[
+ |f(1)-f(0)|\le\tfrac12m(m-1)\|D\|_2^2.
+\]
+Apply Lemma~\ref{density-projection-bound}.  The case $m=1$
+is exact and needs no differentiation.  The adjacency kernel of a
+finite simple host, assigned zero on repeated coordinates, proves
+the finite-host assertion without an asymptotic error.
+
+For sharpness let $r=2n+1$, take independent uniform signs, and set
+$U=\mathbf1_{\{X_1+\cdots+X_r>0\}}$.  Then $p=1/2$ and
+\[
+ d(\pm1)=\frac12\pm\frac12\frac{\binom{2n}{n}}{4^n}.
+\]
+As $P_3$ has two edges,
+\[
+ t(P_3^{[r]},U)-\frac14
+  =\operatorname{Var}(d)
+  =\frac14\left(\frac{\binom{2n}{n}}{4^n}\right)^2
+  \sim\frac{1}{2\pi r},
+\]
+where the last assertion follows from Stirling's formula.
+\end{proof}
+
+\begin{theorem}[Girth-sensitive approximation under regularity]
+\label{density-regular-girth}
+Let $F$ be a finite simple graph with $m\ge1$ edges, and let
+$U:\Omega^r\to[0,1]$ be symmetric, where $r\ge2$, with every
+one-coordinate marginal equal to its mean $p$.
+If $F$ is a forest, then $t(F^{[r]},U)=p^m$ exactly.
+Otherwise, if $g$ is the girth of $F$, then
+\begin{equation}\label{density-girth-error}
+ \left|t(F^{[r]},U)-p^m\right|
+ \le (2^m-1)
+       \left(\frac{2p(1-p)}{r(r-1)}\right)^{g/2}.
+\end{equation}
+The same assertion holds for finite regular $r$-uniform hosts,
+with $p=p_r(G)$.  For every fixed cyclic graph $F$, the order
+$r^{-g}$ is optimal among such kernels, already at density $p=1/2$.
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+Put $D=M_U-p$ and
+$q=(2p(1-p)/(r(r-1)))^{1/2}$.  Both one-variable integrals of $D$
+are zero, $|D|\le1$, and Lemma~\ref{density-projection-bound}
+gives $\|D\|_2\le q\le1$.  Expand
+\[
+ t(F,M_U)-p^m
+  =\sum_{\varnothing\ne A\subseteq E(F)}
+       p^{m-|A|}t(F_A,D),\qquad F_A=(V(F),A).
+\]
+A vertex of degree one in $F_A$ makes its term vanish by integration.
+Every other nonempty $F_A$ contains a cycle $C$ of length
+$\ell\ge g$.  After taking absolute values, discard the factors
+outside $C$ to obtain
+\[
+ |t(F_A,D)|\le t(C,|D|)\le\|D\|_2^\ell\le q^g.
+\]
+For the middle inequality, the symmetric kernel $|D|$ defines a
+self-adjoint operator on the finite-dimensional space $L^2(\Omega)$.
+If its eigenvalues are $\eta_i$, its cycle integral is
+$\sum_i\eta_i^\ell\le\sum_i|\eta_i|^\ell
+\le(\sum_i\eta_i^2)^{\ell/2}=\|D\|_2^\ell$.
+There are at most $2^m-1$ nonempty edge subsets and
+$p^{m-|A|}\le1$, proving \eqref{density-girth-error}.
+For a forest every nonempty term vanishes.
+The adjacency kernel of a finite regular host has constant
+one-coordinate marginal, so the finite-host statement follows
+without an additional limiting argument.
+
+For sharpness take independent uniform signs, put
+$\theta_r=r^{-1/2}$ and $c_r=\cos\theta_r$, and define
+\[
+ U_r(x_1,\ldots,x_r)
+  =\frac12+\frac14\left[
+       \cos\!\left(\theta_r\sum_{i=1}^r x_i\right)-c_r^r
+     \right].
+\]
+This kernel is symmetric and takes values in $[0,1]$.
+Its cosine term has expectation $c_r^r$.
+Global sign reversal leaves $U_r$ unchanged, so each one-coordinate
+marginal is constant on $\{-1,1\}$.  The mean and these marginals
+are therefore exactly $1/2$.
+Its pair marginal is
+\[
+ M_r(a,b)=\frac12-b_rab,\qquad
+ b_r=\frac14\sin^2\theta_r\,c_r^{r-2}
+       \sim\frac{e^{-1/2}}{4r}.
+\]
+Expanding in the signs shows that only edge subsets with all
+degrees even survive.  If $N_g(F)$ is the number of cycles of
+length $g$ in $F$, the smallest nonempty such subsets are precisely
+these cycles.  Consequently
+\[
+ t(F^{[r]},U_r)-2^{-m}
+  =N_g(F)(-b_r)^g2^{-(m-g)}+O_F(b_r^{g+1}).
+\]
+Since $N_g(F)>0$, the absolute difference is of order $r^{-g}$.
+In particular, for $F=C_g$ the identity is exactly
+$t(C_g^{[r]},U_r)-2^{-g}=(-b_r)^g$.
+\end{proof}
+
+The estimates are uniform over the size of the underlying finite
+probability space.  For the canonical atom $J^{[r]}$, the girth in
+Theorem~\ref{density-regular-girth} is the length of its shortest
+Berge cycle.  Thus the same cyclic structure used in the
+classification controls a quantitative density estimate in regular
+finite hosts.
+
+\subsection{Transfer from proper-subgraph limits}
+
+The preceding estimates do not give the exact Sidorenko inequality.
+For a fixed uniformity the next lemma transfers a strict deficit,
+provided the witness family has sufficiently controlled proper
+edge-subgraph densities.
+
+\begin{lemma}[Transfer from proper-subgraph limits]
+\label{density-transfer}
+Let $J$ be a finite graph with $m\ge1$ edges.
+Suppose there are finite symmetric nonnegative kernels $V_q$ with
+constant row integral one such that
+\[
+ t(J_A,V_q)\longrightarrow1\quad(A\subsetneq E(J)),\qquad
+ t(J,V_q)\longrightarrow a<1,
+\]
+where $J_A=(V(J),A)$.
+Then, for every integer $r\ge2$, there is a symmetric
+$[0,1]$-valued $r$-kernel $W$ with $0<p(W)<1$ and
+\[
+ t(J^{[r]},W)<p(W)^m.
+\]
+There is also a finite simple $r$-uniform host with the same strict
+inequality.
+\end{lemma}
+\begin{proof}
+For any fixed $0<\lambda\le1$, expansion over subsets of $E(J)$ gives
+\begin{align*}
+ t(J,1+\lambda(V_q-1))
+ &=\sum_{A\subseteq E(J)}
+       \lambda^{|A|}(1-\lambda)^{m-|A|}t(J_A,V_q)\\
+ &\longrightarrow 1+\lambda^m(a-1)<1.
+\end{align*}
+Apply Lemma~\ref{density-matching} with $\lambda=\lambda_r$.
+By \eqref{density-expansion-identity}, the resulting $U_r$ has mean
+one and $t(J^{[r]},U_r)<1$ for all sufficiently large $q$.
+Here $r$ is fixed before $q$ is chosen; the limiting deficit is
+$(1-a)\lambda_r^m$, so the required index may depend on $r$.
+Choose such a $q$, let $L=\max U_r$, and put $W=U_r/L$.
+The strict inequality implies that $U_r$ is nonconstant; its mean is
+one, so $L>1$.  Homogeneity gives the required inequality with
+$p(W)=1/L\in(0,1)$.
+
+For the finite-host assertion, sample $n$ independent labels from the
+finite probability space, and include each $r$-set independently,
+conditional on those labels, with probability given by $W$.
+Let $G_n$ be the resulting simple $r$-uniform hypergraph and write
+$F=J^{[r]}$, $v=|V(F)|$, $p=p(W)$, and $\tau=t(F,W)<p^m$.
+An injective vertex map from $F$ uses distinct target edges, whence
+\[
+ \mathbb E t(F,G_n)\le \tau+\binom v2/n,\qquad
+ \mathbb E p_r(G_n)=\frac{(n)_r}{n^r}p.
+ \]
+Consequently, for sufficiently large $n$,
+\[
+ \mathbb E t(F,G_n)<\bigl(\mathbb E p_r(G_n)\bigr)^m
+             \le \mathbb E\bigl[p_r(G_n)^m\bigr],
+\]
+the final inequality being Jensen's inequality.
+Some realization therefore has $t(F,G_n)<p_r(G_n)^m$.
+\end{proof}
+
+\subsection{The source pattern and its proper-subgraph limits}
+
+Let $\mathcal T$ be the following $22$ triples on
+$P=\{0,\ldots,12\}$, in the order used by \citep{openai161}:
+\[
+\begin{array}{llll}
+\{0,1,3\}&\{0,1,9\}&\{0,2,3\}&\{0,2,9\}\\
+\{1,3,10\}&\{1,7,10\}&\{1,7,12\}&\{1,9,12\}\\
+\{2,3,4\}&\{2,4,9\}&\{3,4,11\}&\{3,10,11\}\\
+\{4,8,9\}&\{4,8,11\}&\{5,6,7\}&\{5,6,11\}\\
+\{5,7,10\}&\{5,10,11\}&\{6,7,12\}&\{6,8,11\}\\
+\{6,8,12\}&\{8,9,12\}.&&
+\end{array}
+\]
+Let $J$ be the bipartite incidence graph between $P$ and $\mathcal T$.
+It has $35$ vertices and $66$ edges.  Every point pair occurring in a
+face occurs in exactly two faces, and the graph joining faces that
+share a pair is connected.  These are the first assertions of the finite-complex proposition
+in \citep[Section~2]{openai161}, and are also directly checkable
+from the displayed list.
+
+For a rectangular kernel $W:\mathcal X\times\mathcal Y\to[0,\infty)$
+and $B\subseteq E(J)$, the notation $t_{P,\mathcal T}(J_B,W)$
+means that the vertices in $P$ are sampled from $\mathcal X$ and
+those in $\mathcal T$ from $\mathcal Y$, independently.  Write
+$W^\top(y,x)=W(x,y)$ for the transpose.
+
+\begin{proposition}[The source supplies the transfer hypotheses]
+\label{density-source-limits}
+Assume the rank-layer limit stated in \citep[Section~3]{openai161}.
+Then the graph $J$ admits kernels $V_q$ satisfying all hypotheses
+of Lemma~\ref{density-transfer}.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+We keep the source's dimension, type distributions, activation laws,
+thinning probabilities, and positive dilution parameter fixed before
+letting the odd prime $q$ tend to infinity.
+Let $\mathcal K$ be its fixed finite coordinate set.  The matrix
+coordinates range over the additive group of symmetric $D$-by-$D$
+matrices over $\mathbb F_q$, for a fixed sufficiently large even $D$.
+The source uses nonnegative normalized indicators $K_\xi$ of the
+rank-$D/2$ layers with determinant sign $\xi$.  Its rectangular
+kernel has the form
+\[
+ W_q((a,x),(b,y))
+ =\mathbb E_{S,\xi\mid a,b}
+       \prod_{\gamma\in S}K_{\xi_\gamma}(x^\gamma-y^\gamma),
+\]
+where every matrix coordinate is uniform and independent, and
+$\mathbb E K_\xi=1$.  The laws depend on the types but not on the matrices.
+For each fixed $x^\gamma$, the difference $x^\gamma-Y^\gamma$ is
+uniform; the same is true of $X^\gamma-y^\gamma$ for fixed
+$y^\gamma$.  Integrating the product coordinate by coordinate gives
+one for every pair of types.  Averaging over the opposite type shows
+that $W_q$ has row and column integrals identically one, including
+after thinning and dilution.
+
+We next prove the proper-subgraph assertion, which is not a stated
+conclusion of the source's coordinate-expansion lemma.
+Write $\mathscr C=E(J)$ for its source incidences and $\mathcal P$
+for the $33$ point pairs occurring in the faces.
+At a source corner $(i,j)$ there are two pairs $e,f\subset j$
+containing $i$.
+For an activation law on a coordinate set $S$ and signs $\xi$, set
+\[
+ \phi_\alpha=\mathbf1_{\{\alpha\subseteq S\}}
+                   \prod_{\gamma\in\alpha}\xi_\gamma,\qquad
+ A(\alpha,\beta)=\mathbb E(\phi_\alpha\phi_\beta).
+\]
+Here $\alpha,\beta\subseteq\mathcal K$, and $\phi_\alpha$ is defined
+to be zero when $\alpha\not\subseteq S$.  The source's laws have
+$\mathbb E\phi_\alpha=0$ for every nonempty
+$\alpha$, so $A(\alpha,\varnothing)=A(\varnothing,\alpha)=0$ for
+nonempty $\alpha$.
+
+Fix $B\subsetneq\mathscr C$ and a type assignment.
+In the proof of the coordinate-expansion lemma, force the activation
+at every omitted source incidence in $\mathscr C\setminus B$ to be
+$S=\varnothing$, and retain the original laws at the other incidences.
+These modified laws may depend on the individual source incidence,
+even when two incidences have the same type pair; we therefore repeat
+the proof, rather than invoke its type-pair statement verbatim.
+After independently sampling the law at each incidence, each
+coordinate has some active incidence set to which the source's
+rank-layer lemma applies.  That lemma is uniform over every subset
+of $\mathscr C$ and every sign choice.  There are finitely many
+configurations, so its $o(1)$ error survives the finite products and
+averages exactly as in the original proof.
+The resulting limiting expression is
+\[
+ \sum_{\substack{\alpha_e\subseteq\mathcal K\\(e\in\mathcal P)}}
+       \prod_{(i,j)\in\mathscr C}
+       A_{i,j}(\alpha_{e(i,j)},\alpha_{f(i,j)}).
+\]
+At an omitted corner the factor is zero unless both adjacent pair
+labels are empty.  At every other corner an empty label and a
+nonempty label also give zero by the first-moment identity.
+Hence, in any nonzero term, emptiness propagates to all three pair
+labels of a face and then across every shared-pair adjacency.
+There is an omitted corner, and the face-neighbor graph is
+connected, so every pair label must be empty.
+The all-empty term equals one.
+After averaging over the finitely many type assignments, this proves
+\[
+ t_{P,\mathcal T}(J_B,W_q)\longrightarrow1.
+ \]
+The reversed-difference version of the rank-layer lemma gives the
+same conclusion for the transposed rectangular kernel.
+
+For the full incidence set, the source's signed coefficient and
+dilution construction instead gives
+\[
+ t_{P,\mathcal T}(J,W_q)\longrightarrow
+    1+\varepsilon^{13}t_0,\qquad
+ t_{P,\mathcal T}(J,W_q^\top)\longrightarrow
+    1+\varepsilon^{22}t_1,
+\]
+where $t_0<0$, and $\varepsilon>0$ is fixed small enough that the
+product of these two limits is some $a<1$.
+These are the dilution limits and strict product inequality
+proved in \citep[Section~3]{openai161}.
+
+On the product probability space of the two rectangular sides, put
+\[
+ V_q((x,y),(x',y'))=W_q(x,y')W_q(x',y).
+\]
+This is symmetric and nonnegative.  Its row integral is one by the
+exact row and column identities for $W_q$.
+For any $B\subseteq\mathscr C$, bipartiteness separates the variables
+in the two factors, giving
+\[
+ t(J_B,V_q)=
+ t_{P,\mathcal T}(J_B,W_q)\,
+ t_{P,\mathcal T}(J_B,W_q^\top).
+\]
+Thus every proper $B$ has limit one, and the full pattern has limit
+$a<1$, as required.
+\end{proof}
+
+\subsection{Obligatory atoms without a Sidorenko expansion}
+
+\begin{theorem}[An obligatory atom below the random-density bound]
+\label{density-obligatory-atom}
+Assume the rank-layer limit in \citep[Section~3]{openai161}.
+For every integer $r\ge3$, the hypergraph $A_r=J^{[r]}$ is an
+obligatory canonical cyclic atom with
+\[
+ |E(A_r)|=66,\qquad |V(A_r)|=66r-97,\qquad
+ \beta(A_r)=32.
+\]
+Here $\beta$ is the cycle rank of the connected Levi graph.
+Nevertheless, there is a finite simple $r$-uniform host $G$ with
+\[
+ t(A_r,G)<p_r(G)^{66}.
+\]
+Here obligatoriness concerns injective non-induced containment in
+every uncountably chromatic host, whereas $t$ counts all
+homomorphisms in a finite host.
+\end{theorem}
+\begin{proof}
+First $J$ is $2$-connected.
+After deleting a point-node, each pair of neighboring faces still
+shares at least the other point of its common pair.  Connectivity
+of the face-neighbor graph therefore connects all remaining face
+nodes, and every remaining point attaches to one of them.
+After deleting a face-node $j=\{a,b,c\}$, the other face on each of
+$\{a,b\}$, $\{a,c\}$ and $\{b,c\}$ provides a two-edge incidence
+path connecting that pair of points while avoiding $j$.
+Thus the three neighbors of $j$ remain in one component.
+Replacing every passage through $j$ by these paths shows that
+$J-j$ remains connected.
+Consequently no vertex of $J$ is a cut vertex.
+
+The graph is bipartite, so its private expansion is obligatory by
+Reiher's bipartite-expansion theorem \cite[Theorem 1.2]{reiher2024}.
+Since the core $J$ is $2$-connected,
+Corollary~\ref{uniform-canonical-atoms} identifies $A_r$ as one
+cyclic atom.  Equivalently, its cyclic incidence block is the
+subdivision of $J$, and all its other incidence edges go to the
+private degree-one vertices.
+The counts follow from the expansion:
+\[
+ |V(A_r)|=35+66(r-2),\qquad
+ \beta(A_r)=(r-1)66-|V(A_r)|+1=32.
+\]
+Finally apply Proposition~\ref{density-source-limits} and
+Lemma~\ref{density-transfer}.
+\end{proof}
+
+\begin{corollary}[Failure of the expansion conjecture]
+\label{density-nie-spiro}
+Under the rank-layer hypothesis of
+Theorem~\ref{density-obligatory-atom}, there is a bipartite graph
+$J$ such that $J^{[r]}$ is not Sidorenko for every integer $r\ge2$.
+Consequently Conjecture~5.1 of Nie--Spiro \cite{niespiro2025} is false.
+\end{corollary}
+\begin{proof}
+Use the same $35$-vertex graph $J$.
+For $r\ge3$, apply Theorem~\ref{density-obligatory-atom}.
+The case $r=2$ follows from Lemma~\ref{density-transfer}, or directly
+from the source counterexample, since $J^{[2]}=J$.
+The private expansion used by Nie--Spiro is exactly $J^{[r]}$ here.  Their Conjecture~5.1 asserts that every
+bipartite graph has a Sidorenko expansion at some rank $r\ge2$;
+this one graph has none.
+\end{proof}
+
+This corollary does not settle the general converse question posed
+in Nie--Spiro's Question~5.2.  The proof uses the additional
+proper-subgraph limits of this particular graph's witness family,
+not a transfer theorem for every non-Sidorenko graph.
+
+\subsection{Equality at the random benchmark does not force other densities}
+
+The density deficit has a further consequence.  Interpolation adapts
+the graph argument in \citep[Section~8]{openai161} to the actual
+hypergraph kernels constructed above.  We state the consequence as
+three explicit limits.
+
+\begin{corollary}[Two densities do not determine the four-cycle density]
+\label{density-forcing-separation}
+Under the rank-layer hypothesis of Theorem~\ref{density-obligatory-atom},
+for every $r\ge3$ there are $p\in(0,1)$ and finite simple
+$r$-uniform hosts $G_n$ of orders tending to infinity such that
+\[
+ p_r(G_n)\longrightarrow p,\qquad
+ t(A_r,G_n)\longrightarrow p^{66},
+\]
+but
+\[
+ t(C_4^{[r]},G_n)\longrightarrow q>p^4.
+\]
+Thus matching the edge and $A_r$ densities of a constant kernel
+does not determine even the density of the obligatory expansion
+$C_4^{[r]}$.
+\end{corollary}
+\begin{proof}
+Write $Q_U=M_U$ for the pair marginal in
+\eqref{density-expansion-identity}.
+Take the negative kernel $U_-$ of mean $p\in(0,1)$ from
+Lemma~\ref{density-transfer}, and let $Q_-=Q_{U_-}$.
+It has mean $p$ and is nonconstant, since otherwise
+$t(A_r,U_-)=t(J,Q_-)=p^{66}$.
+
+On the balanced sign space $\{-1,1\}$ choose
+\[
+ 0<\delta\le \frac{\min(p,1-p)}{\binom r2},
+ \qquad
+ U_+(z_1,\ldots,z_r)
+   =p+\delta\sum_{1\le a<b\le r}z_a z_b.
+\]
+This kernel is symmetric, takes values in $[0,1]$, and has mean $p$.
+Its pair marginal is
+$Q_+(a,b)=p+\delta ab$: every other summand contains an independently
+integrated sign to its first power.
+Expansion and sign averaging therefore give
+\[
+ t(A_r,U_+)=t(J,Q_+)
+ =\sum_{\substack{A\subseteq E(J)\\
+                    d_A(v)\text{ even for every }v}}
+             p^{66-|A|}\delta^{|A|}
+ >p^{66}.
+\]
+The empty set contributes $p^{66}$; all terms are nonnegative, and
+the edge set of a cycle contributes positively.  The graph $J$
+has a cycle because it has $66$ edges and only $35$ vertices.
+
+Lift $U_-$ and $U_+$ to the independent coordinates of the product
+of their finite probability spaces and put
+\[
+ U_s=(1-s)U_-+sU_+,\qquad 0\le s\le1.
+\]
+Every $U_s$ is symmetric, $[0,1]$-valued, and has mean $p$.
+The function $s\mapsto t(A_r,U_s)$ is a polynomial with endpoint
+values strictly below and above $p^{66}$.
+Thus some $s_*\in(0,1)$ gives equality.
+Its pair marginal is
+\[
+ Q_s((x,a),(y,b))=(1-s)Q_-(x,y)+s(p+\delta ab).
+\]
+The centered summands depend on independent coordinate pairs and
+both have mean zero.  Orthogonality gives
+\[
+ \|Q_s-p\|_2^2
+  =(1-s)^2\|Q_--p\|_2^2+s^2\delta^2>0
+ \qquad(0\le s\le1).
+\]
+In particular $Q=Q_{s_*}$ is nonconstant.
+
+For completeness, every finite symmetric nonnegative kernel $Q$
+of mean $p$ satisfies $t(C_4,Q)\ge p^4$, with equality only when
+$Q$ is constant.  Indeed, put
+$d(x)=\mathbb E_zQ(x,z)$ and $c(x,y)=\mathbb E_zQ(x,z)Q(y,z)$.
+Two applications of Cauchy--Schwarz give
+\[
+ t(C_4,Q)=\mathbb E_{x,y}c(x,y)^2
+   \ge\bigl(\mathbb E_z d(z)^2\bigr)^2\ge p^4.
+\]
+Equality forces $d(x)=p$ and $c(x,y)=p^2$ on every positive-mass
+atom and atom pair.  Diagonal pairs then give
+\[
+ \mathbb E_z(Q(x,z)-p)^2=c(x,x)-2p\,d(x)+p^2=0.
+\]
+Thus equality forces constancy.  Applied to the present $Q$, this
+shows
+\[
+ t(C_4^{[r]},U_{s_*})=t(C_4,Q)>p^4.
+\]
+
+It remains to pass to finite simple hosts with all three limits.
+Sample $n$ independent vertex labels and then, conditionally
+independently, include each $r$-set with probability $U_{s_*}$ of
+its labels.  For every fixed finite simple $r$-uniform $A$,
+injective maps give the kernel density, and the fraction of vertex
+maps having a collision is $O_A(n^{-1})$.  Consequently
+\[
+ \mathbb E t(A,G_n)=t(A,U_{s_*})+O_A(n^{-1}).
+\]
+The contributions from two vertex maps are independent whenever
+their images are disjoint.  Overlapping pairs form an
+$O_A(n^{-1})$ fraction, so
+$\operatorname{Var}(t(A,G_n))=O_A(n^{-1})$.
+Chebyshev's inequality with tolerance $n^{-1/4}$, followed by a
+union bound for the single edge, $A_r$, and $C_4^{[r]}$, gives
+positive probability that all three desired approximations hold
+for each sufficiently large $n$.
+Choose one such realization for each $n$.
+These deterministic hosts have the three asserted limits.
+\end{proof}
+
+
+The density $p$ in this corollary can depend on $r$.  Both test
+patterns are obligatory, yet the equality for one leaves a strict
+excess for the other.  This compares their finite homomorphism
+densities with the constant-kernel benchmark.
+
+The earlier linear non-Sidorenko examples of
+\citet[Theorem~3.1]{conlonleesidorenko2024} include systems of odd
+girth.  The present $A_r$ has only even Berge cycles, satisfies the
+bridge criterion, and is one canonical atom.  Its obstruction lies
+within the obligatory class.  The graph counterexample and its
+finite-field construction are due to \citet{openai161}; the
+additional conclusions here come from the proper-subgraph limits,
+matching transfer, and canonical identification.
+
